You create background jobs with the [background Liquid tag](/developer-guide/background-jobs/creating-background-job-liquid). Once they're created, you can manage them with GraphQL:

* [`admin_background_jobs`](/api-reference/graphql/admin/queries/admin-background-jobs) lists jobs.
* [`admin_background_job_stop`](/api-reference/graphql/admin/mutations/admin-background-job-stop) stops a job that is running.
* [`admin_background_job_delete`](/api-reference/graphql/admin/mutations/admin-background-job-delete) deletes one job.
* [`admin_background_jobs_delete_all`](/api-reference/graphql/admin/mutations/admin-background-jobs-delete-all) deletes the jobs that match a filter.
* [`admin_background_job_retry`](/api-reference/graphql/admin/mutations/admin-background-job-retry) runs a failed job again.

You can run these from Liquid with the [graphql tag](/api-reference/liquid/platformos-tags#graphql), or from the [pos-cli GUI](/developer-guide/pos-cli/developing-graphql-queries-using-pos-cli-gui).

{% include 'alert/note', content: 'There is no GraphQL mutation that creates a background job. To run GraphQL in the background, put the `graphql` tag inside a background job. See [Running GraphQL in a background job](/developer-guide/background-jobs/creating-background-job-liquid#running-graphql-in-a-background-job).' %}

## Job types

Every job is in one of the following states, which you choose with the `type` filter:

| Type        | Jobs |
|-------------|------|
| `SCHEDULED` | Jobs scheduled to run in the future, using `delay`. This is the default. |
| `RUNNING`   | Jobs being processed right now. |
| `RETRY`     | Jobs that failed and are waiting to be retried. |
| `DEAD`      | Jobs that failed and won't be retried any more. |

A job scheduled without a `delay` goes straight to the queue and doesn't appear as `SCHEDULED`. You will see it as `RUNNING` while it runs.

## Listing background jobs

This query returns the scheduled jobs with a given `source_name`:

```graphql
{% raw %}
# app/graphql/background_jobs/search.graphql
query search_background_jobs($source_name: String!) {
  admin_background_jobs(
    per_page: 100
    filter: {
      type: SCHEDULED
      source_name: { value: $source_name }
    }
  ) {
    total_entries
    results {
      id
      source_name
      queue
      run_at
      arguments
    }
  }
}
{% endraw %}
```

```liquid
{% raw %}
{% liquid
  graphql jobs = 'background_jobs/search', source_name: 'daily-report'
  log jobs, type: 'background-jobs'
%}
{% endraw %}
```

The `id` of a job is the value that the `background` tag assigned to its variable (`job_id` in `background job_id = 'jobs/my_job'`).

You can also filter jobs by:

* `id`: a single job.
* `timestamp`: the time of the job's next step. It filters by `run_at` for `SCHEDULED` jobs, by `retry_at` for `RETRY` jobs, and by `dead_at` for `DEAD` jobs. For example, `timestamp: { lt: "2026-12-01" }` returns jobs scheduled to run before December 1st.
* `or`: a list of filters, of which at least one has to match.

The `source_name` filter is case-sensitive. Only `type`, `id`, `source_name`, `timestamp`, and `or` filter jobs. The other fields of the filter have no effect.

To find out why jobs failed, query the `DEAD` jobs and read their `error_class` and `error_message` fields.

The query returns up to 100 jobs per page and up to 50 pages.

## Deleting a background job

To cancel a single job, delete it by its ID:

```graphql
{% raw %}
# app/graphql/background_jobs/delete.graphql
mutation delete_background_job($id: ID!) {
  admin_background_job_delete(id: $id) {
    id
  }
}
{% endraw %}
```

You can delete jobs that are `SCHEDULED`, `RETRY`, or `DEAD`. A job that is already running can't be deleted, but you can [stop it](#stopping-a-running-background-job).

## Deleting many background jobs

To delete all jobs that match a filter, use `admin_background_jobs_delete_all`. It takes the same filter as `admin_background_jobs`:

```graphql
{% raw %}
# app/graphql/background_jobs/delete_all.graphql
mutation delete_background_jobs($source_name: String!) {
  admin_background_jobs_delete_all(
    filter: {
      type: SCHEDULED
      source_name: { value: $source_name }
    }
  ) {
    results {
      id
    }
  }
}
{% endraw %}
```

{% include 'alert/important', content: 'One call deletes at most 100 jobs. If more jobs match your filter, call the mutation again until `results` is empty.' %}

## Stopping a running background job

To stop a job that is running right now, use its ID:

```graphql
{% raw %}
# app/graphql/background_jobs/stop.graphql
mutation stop_background_job($id: ID!) {
  admin_background_job_stop(id: $id) {
    id
  }
}
{% endraw %}
```

You can also stop a job from the [pos-cli GUI](/developer-guide/pos-cli/developing-graphql-queries-using-pos-cli-gui): open **Background jobs**, set **Type** to **Running**, and click the stop button next to the job.

Only `RUNNING` jobs can be stopped. To cancel a job that hasn't started yet, [delete it](#deleting-a-background-job) instead. A job that started a few seconds ago may not be listed as running yet. Until it is, `admin_background_job_stop` returns the error `Job not found`, so wait a moment and try again.

The mutation returns as soon as the stop is requested. The job usually stops within a few seconds, and leaves the list of running jobs a few seconds after that.

### What happens when you stop a job

* **It stops wherever it is**, which can be in the middle of its code. This can be between two tags, or while it waits for a database query or an [API call](/developer-guide/notifications/creating-api-call-notification). A query that is running is cancelled. An API call isn't waited for, but the request may already have reached the other service, which can still process it.
* **Changes inside an unfinished transaction are rolled back.** If the job is inside a [`transaction`](/api-reference/liquid/platformos-tags#transaction) tag that hasn't finished, everything the transaction changed is undone, and the background jobs scheduled inside it are never scheduled.
* **Everything else the job has done stays done.** Records saved outside a transaction, emails sent, API calls made, and background jobs scheduled outside a transaction are not undone. Jobs the stopped job has already scheduled still run.
* **`try` doesn't catch the stop.** The `catch` block of a [`try`](/api-reference/liquid/platformos-tags#try) tag doesn't run, but its `ensure` block does.
* **The job isn't retried.** A stopped job doesn't count as failed: it isn't retried, even with `max_attempts`, and it doesn't become `DEAD`.
* **The stop is logged.** The [logs](/best-practices/qa/creating-application-logs) get an entry of type `BackgroundJobStopped` with the message `Background job <id> stopped with admin_background_job_stop`.

{% include 'alert/important', content: 'A job that schedules its own next run stops its chain if you stop it before it schedules the next run. This is another reason to schedule the next run at the beginning of the job. See [Scheduling Recurring Background Jobs](/developer-guide/background-jobs/scheduling-recurring-background-jobs).' %}

A job is safest to stop when stopping it at any point leaves your data consistent. Do work that has to happen all together inside a `transaction`, and write long jobs so that you can run them again after a stop, for example by processing records in batches and skipping the ones that are already done.

### Stopping a deploy, data operation, or Instance copy

Deploys, data exports, imports and cleans, and copying an Instance also run as background jobs of your Instance, so they are listed as running jobs too, and you can stop them:

* **Deploy**: ends with the status `stopped`, and `pos-cli deploy` reports `Deploy stopped`. Depending on when it was stopped, some of its changes may already be in place, so deploy again to finish it. A [migration](/developer-guide/data-import-export/migrating-data) that was running when you stopped the deploy is marked as failed, so it runs again with the next deploy, or when you run it with `pos-cli migrations run`.
* **Data export and data import**: end with the status `stopped`, and pos-cli reports `Export stopped` or `Import stopped`. Records imported before the stop may stay imported, so check your data before you import it again.
* **Copying an Instance**: the copy fails, with the stop as the reason.
* **Data clean**: `pos-cli data clean` reports that the cleanup failed.

## Retrying a failed background job

A job that failed and won't be retried any more is `DEAD`. To run it again, retry it by its ID:

```graphql
{% raw %}
# app/graphql/background_jobs/retry.graphql
mutation retry_background_job($id: ID!) {
  admin_background_job_retry(id: $id) {
    id
  }
}
{% endraw %}
```

Only `DEAD` jobs can be retried this way.

